<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-all-three-cases" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>All Three Cases of the Characteristic Equation</title>

  <p>
    When solving <m>ay'' + by' + cy = 0</m>, we always start by looking at the
    characteristic equation <m>ar^2 + br + c = 0</m>. We saw exactly how this played out
    in <xref ref="ex-char-eq-distinct"/> when there were two distinct real roots. Unfortunately,
    we won't always be in this case. Recall from algebra that the roots of a
    quadratic can fall into three cases:
    <ul>
      <li><p>Two distinct real roots <m>r_1 \neq r_2</m></p></li>
      <li><p>One repeated real root <m>r_1 = r_2</m></p></li>
      <li><p>Two complex conjugate roots <m>\alpha \pm \beta i</m></p></li>
    </ul>
    The observation from the previous page only covers the first case.
    When there is only one real root, that gives us just one solution of the
    form <m>e^{rx}</m>, which isn't enough to form the general solution.
    And when there are complex roots, <m>e^{r_1 x}</m> and <m>e^{r_2 x}</m> would
    be complex-valued, which is not what we want in this course. We will need to extract 
    two real-valued solutions from the complex ones in that case.
  </p>

  <p>
    For now, we'll just state what to do in all three cases in the theorem below to get some quick practice. We will
    provide justification for cases 2 and 3 on the following pages. In this page, we are just getting acquainted 
    with cases two and three before we move on to some more interesting problems!
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-three-cases-char-eq">
    <title>Solving <m>ay'' + by' + cy = 0</m>: All Three Cases</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the characteristic equation <m>ar^2 + br + c = 0</m>. In each case below, we find two 
        linearly independent solutions <m>y_1</m> and <m>y_2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Two distinct real roots</alert> <m>r_1 \neq r_2</m>:
              <me>y_1 = e^{r_1 x}, \qquad y_2 = e^{r_2 x}</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>One repeated real root</alert> <m>r_1</m>:
              <me>y_1 = e^{r_1 x}, \qquad y_2 = xe^{r_1 x}</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <alert>Two complex conjugate roots</alert> <m>\alpha \pm \beta i</m>:
              <me>y_1 = e^{\alpha x}\cos(\beta x), \qquad y_2 = e^{\alpha x}\sin(\beta x)</me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        In each case, the <term>general solution</term> is <m>y = c_1 y_1 + c_2 y_2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    The next example demonstrates the repeated root case.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-repeated-root">
    <title>Solving <m>y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the general solution to <m>y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Notice the characteristic equation is <m>r^2 - 6r + 9 = 0</m>.
        This factors as <m>(r-3)^2 = 0</m>, meaning the factor <m>(r-3)</m>
        shows up <em>twice</em>, so this is a repeated root.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The characteristic equation is <m>r^2 - 6r + 9 = 0</m>, which factors as:
        <me>(r - 3)^2 = 0.</me>
        This gives a single repeated root <m>r_1 = 3</m>. By the theorem, the two
        linearly independent solutions are:
        <me>y_1 = e^{3x} \qquad \text{and} \qquad y_2 = xe^{3x}.</me>
        So the general solution is:
        <me>y = c_1 e^{3x} + c_2 xe^{3x}.</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    We end with applying the theorem to the complex root case.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-complex-roots">
    <title>Solving <m>y'' + 4y' + 5y = 0</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the general solution to <m>y'' + 4y' + 5y = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Notice the characteristic equation is <m>r^2 + 4r + 5 = 0</m>.
        This does not factor easily, so use the quadratic formula:
        <me>r = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.</me>
        You should find complex roots in this case!
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The characteristic equation is <m>r^2 + 4r + 5 = 0</m>. Applying the
        quadratic formula with <m>a = 1</m>, <m>b = 4</m>, <m>c = 5</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>r \amp= \frac{-4 \pm \sqrt{16 - 20}}{2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{-4 \pm \sqrt{-4}}{2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{-4 \pm 2i}{2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= -2 \pm i.</mrow>
        </md>
        So we have complex roots with <m>\alpha = -2</m> and <m>\beta = 1</m>.
        By the theorem, the two linearly independent solutions are:
        <me>y_1 = e^{-2x}\cos(x) \qquad \text{and} \qquad y_2 = e^{-2x}\sin(x).</me>
        So the general solution is:
        <me>y = e^{-2x}\left(c_1\cos(x) + c_2\sin(x)\right).</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-complex-roots-identify">
    <statement>
      <p>
        After applying the quadratic formula to a characteristic equation, you find:
        <me>r = \frac{4 \pm \sqrt{-12}}{2} = 2 \pm i\sqrt{3}.</me>
        What is the general solution?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>y = e^{2x}\left(c_1\cos(\sqrt{3}\,x) + c_2\sin(\sqrt{3}\,x)\right)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Exactly! From <m>r = 2 \pm i\sqrt{3}</m> we read off <m>\alpha = 2</m> and
            <m>\beta = \sqrt{3}</m>. The general solution is
            <m>y = e^{\alpha x}(c_1\cos(\beta x) + c_2\sin(\beta x)) = e^{2x}(c_1\cos(\sqrt{3}\,x) + c_2\sin(\sqrt{3}\,x))</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>y = e^{\sqrt{3}\,x}\left(c_1\cos(2x) + c_2\sin(2x)\right)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close, but <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\beta</m> are swapped. From
            <m>r = 2 \pm i\sqrt{3}</m>, the real part is <m>\alpha = 2</m> (goes in
            the exponent) and the imaginary part is <m>\beta = \sqrt{3}</m> (goes inside
            the trig functions).
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>y = c_1\cos(\sqrt{3}\,x) + c_2\sin(\sqrt{3}\,x)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Don't forget the <m>e^{\alpha x}</m> factor out front! Since <m>\alpha = 2 \neq 0</m>,
            we need <m>e^{2x}</m> multiplying the trig terms.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>y = c_1 e^{(2 + i\sqrt{3})x} + c_2 e^{(2 - i\sqrt{3})x}</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This would be the solution if we used the complex roots directly, but we
            want a real-valued solution. The theorem tells us to use
            <m>e^{\alpha x}\cos(\beta x)</m> and <m>e^{\alpha x}\sin(\beta x)</m> instead.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Distinct real roots</alert> <m>r_1 \neq r_2</m>: general solution
          <m>y = c_1 e^{r_1 x} + c_2 e^{r_2 x}</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Repeated root</alert> <m>r_1</m>: general solution
          <m>y = c_1 e^{r_1 x} + c_2 x e^{r_1 x}</m>. 
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Complex roots</alert> <m>\alpha \pm \beta i</m>: general solution
          <m>y = e^{\alpha x}(c_1\cos(\beta x) + c_2\sin(\beta x))</m>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <remark xml:id="remark-no-video-3-1-3">
    <p>
      There is no separate video for this page.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-1-3" iframe="question-form.html?section=3.1.3" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>

</section>